<!-- Part 14 e2e scenario: a ModellingRule-only override, the negative space
     around test_vt_override.NodeSet2 (which changes the TypeDefinition) and
     test_vt_contradiction.NodeSet2 (which changes ValueRank). Here NOTHING
     about the declaration's own content changes: BaseType declares Optional
     "Setting" : PropertyType/Int32 (scalar, default ValueRank). SubType, a
     subtype of BaseType, redeclares "Setting" with the exact same
     TypeDefinition/DataType/ValueRank, only tightening the ModellingRule
     from Optional to Mandatory.

     Per _resolve_target's needs_vt test (type/valuerank/datatype/local-
     children), none of those changed, so SubType's "Setting" is expected to
     resolve to the SAME Virtual Type as BaseType's, not mint a second one:
     the need-based design's central claim is that a Virtual Type is only
     minted when a declaration's own content is genuinely new, and a
     ModellingRule is not "content" in that sense. What DOES have to change
     is the restriction written on SubType itself: _needs_owner_restriction
     checks is_optional too, so SubType gets its own
     minQualifiedCardinality(1) restriction pointing at that same shared
     Virtual Type, on top of the allValuesFrom restriction both types share.
     Expect no contradiction: this is a legal, common OPC UA pattern
     (a subtype promising a component the base type only offered). -->
